-- title:
--   Theorem 8 (24) — ξ^K(Y, X) ≥ ξ^K(Y, U) = ξ^K(Y, X|σ(g)), with equality for injective g
-- statement:
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal B,\mathbb P)$ be a probability space, $\mathsf X,\mathsf Y,\mathsf U$ Polish spaces, $X:\Omega\to\mathsf X$ and $Y:\Omega\to\mathsf Y$ measurable with $\mu$ the law of $X$, and $\mathsf k:\mathsf Y\times\mathsf Y\to[0,+\infty]$ continuous with $\mathsf k(y,y')=0\iff y=y'$. Let $g:\mathsf X\to\mathsf U$ be a Borel map, $U:=g\circ X$, and $\sigma(g)$ the σ-algebra generated by $g$. Then
--
--   1. $$\xi^K(Y,X)\ \ge\ \xi^K(Y,U)\ =\ \xi^K(Y,X\mid\sigma(g));$$
--   2. if $g$ is injective $\mu$-almost everywhere, then $\xi^K(Y,X)=\xi^K(Y,U)$.
--
--   Here $\xi^K(Y,U)=\int_{\mathsf U}K(\nu,\nu'_u)\,d\sigma(u)$ uses a version $\nu'_u$ of the conditional distribution of $Y$ given the random variable $U$, a different object from the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $(X,\sigma(g))$; their equality is part of the statement. All three quantities may be computed with any version.
--
--   Information received through a transformation $U=g(X)$ is worth at most as much as information on $X$ itself, and exactly as much when $g$ is injective.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\sigma(g)$ is `MeasurableSpace.comap g`. "Injective almost everywhere" is read with respect to $\mu$ (the definition `IsInjectiveAE`). Values are in $[0,+\infty]$.
-- source:
--   Borgonovo, Figalli, Plischke & Savaré, Global Sensitivity Analysis via Optimal Transport, authors' manuscript of 7 March 2024 (Management Science, doi:10.1287/mnsc.2023.01796), Theorem 8 (24) and last sentence, p. 11

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_OTSensitivity_Info_Setting
import Definitions.Def_OTSensitivity_Info_InjectiveAE

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology
open scoped ENNReal BoundedContinuousFunction

namespace OTSensitivity.Info

/-- Theorem 8, (24) and its last sentence, p. 11: for a Borel map `g : 𝓧 → 𝓤` into a Polish space,
`U = g ∘ X` and `𝓕 = σ(g)`, `ξ^K(Y, X) ≥ ξ^K(Y, U) = ξ^K(Y, X | σ(g))`; and if `g` is injective
`µ`-almost everywhere, then `ξ^K(Y, X) = ξ^K(Y, U)`. -/
theorem theorem_8_ii {Ω 𝓧 𝓨 𝓤 : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    [TopologicalSpace 𝓧] [PolishSpace 𝓧] [m𝓧 : MeasurableSpace 𝓧] [BorelSpace 𝓧]
    [TopologicalSpace 𝓨] [PolishSpace 𝓨] [MeasurableSpace 𝓨] [BorelSpace 𝓨]
    [TopologicalSpace 𝓤] [PolishSpace 𝓤] [m𝓤 : MeasurableSpace 𝓤] [BorelSpace 𝓤]
    (k : 𝓨 → 𝓨 → ℝ≥0∞) (hk : OTSensitivity.MaxFun.IsCost k)
    (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (X : Ω → 𝓧) (Y : Ω → 𝓨) (hX : Measurable X) (hY : Measurable Y)
    (g : 𝓧 → 𝓤) (hg : Measurable g)
    (κ : Kernel 𝓧 𝓨) (hκ : IsCondDistrib P X Y m𝓧 κ)
    (κU : Kernel 𝓤 𝓨) (hκU : IsCondDistrib P (g ∘ X) Y m𝓤 κU)
    (κg : Kernel 𝓧 𝓨) (hκg : IsCondDistrib P X Y (MeasurableSpace.comap g m𝓤) κg) :
    OTSensitivity.MaxFun.xi k P (g ∘ X) Y κU ≤ OTSensitivity.MaxFun.xi k P X Y κ ∧
      OTSensitivity.MaxFun.xi k P (g ∘ X) Y κU = OTSensitivity.MaxFun.xi k P X Y κg ∧
      (IsInjectiveAE (P.map X) g → OTSensitivity.MaxFun.xi k P X Y κ = OTSensitivity.MaxFun.xi k P (g ∘ X) Y κU) := by sorry

end OTSensitivity.Info
